WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.937
Strong validation starts before writing code.

00:00:02.937 --> 00:00:05.873
Define the expected data contract first.

00:00:05.873 --> 00:00:13.900
That contract should cover type, length, format, range, required fields, optional fields, allowed values, nested structures, and business rules.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:22.710
Without a clear contract, validation tends to become scattered checks that are hard to test and easy to miss during maintenance.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.038
Type is only part of the contract.

00:00:26.038 --> 00:00:31.324
A value may be an integer but still outside the allowed range.

00:00:31.324 --> 00:00:37.785
A string may match a pattern but be too long for storage or business use.

00:00:37.785 --> 00:00:44.246
A list may contain dictionaries, but each item may need required keys and allowed values.

00:00:44.246 --> 00:00:51.098
Python makes nested dictionaries and lists easy to pass around, so nested validation needs deliberate attention.

00:00:51.098 --> 00:00:54.818
Business rules matter as much as technical shape.

00:00:54.818 --> 00:00:57.754
A date range should make sense.

00:00:57.754 --> 00:01:02.257
A status transition should be allowed for the current workflow.

00:01:02.257 --> 00:01:07.151
An account identifier should match the authenticated tenant or user context.

00:01:07.151 --> 00:01:10.088
A discount value should obey policy.

00:01:10.088 --> 00:01:17.723
If validation only checks that fields exist, it may allow well-formed requests that still violate the application model.

00:01:17.723 --> 00:01:21.835
Clear contracts make validation easier to share across teams.

00:01:21.835 --> 00:01:30.645
API schemas, Pydantic models, dataclasses with explicit runtime checks, Django forms, serializer classes, and documented request models can all express expectations.

00:01:30.645 --> 00:01:40.238
The best contract is visible enough for developers, testers, and reviewers to understand what is accepted and what should be rejected before processing.
